Expose a keepalive handle so a response can be completed from another thread.

Crow already supports deferred completion: while a response is incomplete the
read loop stops and records need_to_start_read_after_complete_, so no second
request can land on that connection. What it does not offer is a way to keep
the connection alive across that window.

prepare_buffers() begins by clearing the two lambdas that own the connection:

    res.complete_request_handler_ = nullptr;
    res.is_alive_helper_          = nullptr;
    if (!adaptor_.is_open()) ...

From the original io callback that is safe, because do_read's lambda still
holds `self` on the stack. From a completion posted after that callback has
returned, those two lambdas are the only owners - so clearing them destroys
`this`, and every line after is a use-after-free.

This adds a public std::shared_ptr<void> on crow::response, set alongside the
existing completion handler and cleared alongside it, which a handler can
capture to hold the connection open across an offload. Synchronous handlers
are unaffected: they never touch it, and the member is reset on the same line
as the references it sits with, so it introduces no cycle of its own.

Not yet submitted upstream (https://github.com/CrowCpp/crow).

--- a/include/crow/http_response.h	2026-09-22 13:10:03.946998309 +0200
+++ b/include/crow/http_response.h	2026-09-22 13:10:03.961646627 +0200
@@ -261,6 +261,21 @@
             end();
         }
 
+        /// A strong reference to the connection serving this response.
+        ///
+        /// Capture this in any completion that runs after the originating io
+        /// callback has returned - completing the response from a worker
+        /// thread, for example. Without it, prepare_buffers() clears
+        /// complete_request_handler_ and is_alive_helper_, which by then are
+        /// the connection's only owners, and destroys it before it has
+        /// finished using itself.
+        ///
+        /// Empty for a response not attached to a connection. Cleared in
+        /// prepare_buffers() along with the other self-references, so it does
+        /// not keep the connection alive on its own; a completion that
+        /// captured a copy keeps it alive for exactly as long as it needs to.
+        std::shared_ptr<void> connection_keepalive;
+
         /// Check if the connection is still alive (usually by checking the socket status).
         bool is_alive()
         {

